<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sogolon Condé</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Sogolon Wulen Condé, Sogolon Konte, Sukulung Conté, Sogolon Kédjou</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sogolon Condé was a 13th-century princess of Imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and one of the prominent women of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-sundiata">Epic of Sundiata</a>, the second wife of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghan-kon-fatta">Naré Maghann Konaté</a> and mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita">Sundiata Keita</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>. The epic portrays her as the daughter, or in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griots">griots</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> the younger sister, of the &quot;buffalo woman&quot; Dò Kamissa, ridiculed for her hunchback and for bearing a disabled son. After her husband's death, plots by his first wife Sassouma Bereté drove her into exile with her children until the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mema">Mema</a> gave them asylum. She died in Mema before Sundiata returned to take the throne; griots praise him through her as Sogolon Djata.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mande tradition honours mothers: Sundiata’s name means “<b>Sogolon</b>’s lion”, after the mother whom the epic makes central to his story.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mali-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mali Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>The king marries the hunchbacked <b>Sogolon</b>, and their son Sundiata cannot walk throughout his childhood, mocked by the king's first wife, Sassouma Bereté.</blockquote><a href="/songs/epic-of-sundiata">Epic of Sundiata</a></li></ul></section>
